# Audit — Target B explicit two-largest-tie phase gap

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Candidate:** `docs/reports/target_b_explicit_two_max_phase_gap_2026-09-05.md`  
**Verdict:** PASS under its stated dense-core/coherence hypotheses.  
**Global LEVEL I status:** STILL OPEN.

## 1. Geometric missing floor for the largest pair

If the `A-B` missing density is `mu<=10^(-3)`, the corrected dual-tree theorem gives a complete grid with two-phase omitted population

`L<=3sqrt(mu)+mu+o(1)`.

At `mu=10^(-3)`,

`L<=0.0958683299...`.

Charging the loss once for shorter coherent sums and once for arbitrary omitted-train diagonal cancellation yields

`D^2/b^2>=3-4sqrt(3)L-o(1)`

`>=2.33580-o(1)`.

The full tied component has at most `2b^2+o(b^2)` rhombi, so the lattice comparison gives

`D^2/b^2<2c+o(1)=2.20531558...+o(1)`.

Contradiction. Therefore the largest pair misses at least `10^(-3)b^2-o(b^2)` crossings.

PASS.

## 2. Tied transversal-triple algebra

With

`a=(1-q)b`,

`e_AB=b^2-x`, `e_AC=ba-y`, `e_BC=z`,

the transversal-triple count gives

`bz<=ax+by`.

Hence

`eta b^2=x+y-z>=q x`.

Together with the geometric missing floor this gives

`eta>=(10^(-3)-o(1))q`.

PASS.

## 3. Random balanced restriction

Choose uniformly random subsets `A_0 subset A`, `B_0 subset B` of size `a` and retain all of `C`.

An `A-B` edge survives with probability `(a/b)^2=(1-q)^2`; an edge incident with `C` survives with probability `1-q`, which is larger. Thus

`E[E_0]>=(1-q)^2E`.

Some deterministic choice therefore satisfies this bound. Dividing by

`a^2=(1-q)^2b^2`

gives

`E_0/a^2>=E/b^2=2-q-eta`.

Thus the induced equal-size tripartite graph has balanced defect at most

`e=q+eta`.

PASS.

## 4. Deleted trains are restored explicitly

Under the contradiction assumption `e<10^(-7)`, the explicit balanced combinatorial lemma gives a phase pair in `A_0,B_0,C` with missing density at most

`4sqrt(e)`.

Dual-tree extraction gives a complete grid with internal two-phase omission

`L_grid<=6e^(1/4)+4sqrt(e)+o(1)`.

The restriction deleted exactly

`2(b-a)=2qb`

trains from the original tied component. They are not treated as negligible. Their entire possible diagonal contribution is bounded by their count, giving normalized error

`L_del=2q/(1-q)`

relative to the balanced size `a`.

This is the necessary correction to any shortcut that would simply apply the balanced theorem to the induced graph and forget the deleted original trains.

PASS.

## 5. External errors and the third phase

The unselected balanced phase class has exactly `a` trains and receives its full arbitrary coherent signed cancellation; this is already optimized in `F(1,1)=3`.

Truly non-core train crossings are `o(b^2)=o(a^2)` because `q<10^(-7)`. After a fixed-fraction central trim, penetration depth leaves only `o(a)` truly external diagonal contributors.

PASS.

## 6. Near-balanced numerical contradiction

At `e<=10^(-7)`,

`L_grid<=0.107961676...`,

and

`L_del<=2.000001*10^(-7)`.

Therefore

`D^2/a^2`

`>=3-4sqrt(3)L_grid-2sqrt(3)L_del-o(1)`

`>2.2520-o(1)`.

The full original tied component gives

`D^2/a^2<c(2-e)/(1-q)^2+o(1)`.

For `0<=q<=e<=10^(-7)`, the right side is at most

`2.205317+o(1)`.

Contradiction. Hence

`liminf(q+eta)>=10^(-7)`.

PASS.

## 7. Uniform interpolation

The two established inequalities are

`eta>=(10^(-3)-o(1))q`,

`q+eta>=10^(-7)-o(1)`.

Thus

`q<=eta/(10^(-3)-o(1))`,

and asymptotically

`10^(-7)<=eta(1+10^3)`.

Therefore

`liminf eta>=10^(-10)/1.001`

`=9.99000999...*10^(-11)`.

PASS.

## 8. Scope

The theorem handles exact two-largest-tie class sizes. A general component with `M>b` still requires explicit control of the `M-b` trains before this tied constant can be imported. The local-to-global deficit summation also remains open.
